---
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const install = `pnpm add -D @wesflo/vite-plugin-local-mock-api`;
const fileTree = `mock/
├── PUT_profile.json
└── users/
    ├── profile.json
    └── POST_profile.json`;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<DocsLayout
    title="Overview"
    description="Plug-and-play local API mocks for Vite, with optional manifests and browser controls."
    activePath="/"
>
    <article>
        <p class="kicker">Lockxy · Local Mock Proxy</p>
        <h1>Drop in mock files. Start Vite. Done.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
            Add the plugin and place local response files below your mock directory. Requests are matched automatically
            through naming conventions—no manifest, browser panel, or application-side fetch wrapper is required.
        </p>

        <section class="value-section" aria-labelledby="why-lockxy">
            <h2 id="why-lockxy">Why teams use Lockxy</h2>
            <ul class="value-list">
                <li>
                    <span class="value-icon" aria-hidden="true">⚡</span>
                    <div>
                        <strong>Plug and play</strong>
                        <p>
                            Add the Vite plugin, drop response files into <code>mock/</code>, and keep using the
                            application's real API calls. Naming conventions do the routing.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="value-icon" aria-hidden="true">✈️</span>
                    <div>
                        <strong>Work fully offline</strong>
                        <p>
                            Keep developing on a plane, on a train, or anywhere without a backend, VPN, internet
                            connection, or expensive hotspot.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="value-icon" aria-hidden="true">🔀</span>
                    <div>
                        <strong>Mocks live with the code</strong>
                        <p>
                            Responses are versioned and reviewed in the repository, so frontend and backend developers can
                            inspect shared API assumptions and diagnose new or parallel endpoints.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="value-icon" aria-hidden="true">🔒</span>
                    <div>
                        <strong>Mock data stays local</strong>
                        <p>
                            Lockxy handles mock files on the developer's machine. Requests and responses are not uploaded
                            to an external mock service.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="value-icon" aria-hidden="true">🪙</span>
                    <div>
                        <strong>No service costs</strong>
                        <p>
                            There is no hosted mock API, usage plan, or metered traffic to pay for—regardless of how often
                            the team runs its local mocks.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="value-icon" aria-hidden="true">🛠️</span>
                    <div>
                        <strong>No hosting overhead</strong>
                        <p>
                            No extra server needs to be provisioned, secured, monitored, or maintained just to support
                            local frontend development.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <Code code={install} label="terminal" />

        <div class="cards">
            <a class="card" href={`${base}/getting-started/`}>
                <strong>Get started →</strong>
                <span>Add the plugin, create one mock file, and make your first request.</span>
            </a>
            <a class="card" href={`${base}/configuration/`}>
                <strong>Learn the naming conventions →</strong>
                <span>See how URLs and HTTP methods resolve to local files.</span>
            </a>
            <a class="card" href={`${base}/proxy-panel/`}>
                <strong>Add optional browser controls →</strong>
                <span>Switch manifest scenarios or bypass individual endpoints.</span>
            </a>
        </div>

        <h2 id="plugin-first">Plugin first</h2>
        <p>
            The default workflow is intentionally file-based. For example, <code>GET /api/users/profile</code> resolves to
            <code>mock/users/profile.json</code>. A <code>POST</code> request prefers <code>POST_profile.json</code> and falls
            back to <code>profile.json</code>. In the tree below, that nested generic file also wins for <code>PUT</code>.
            If it is absent, the root-level <code>mock/PUT_profile.json</code> can answer
            <code>PUT /api/users/profile</code>. Directory nesting is useful for specificity, but optional.
        </p>
        <Code code={fileTree} label="project structure" />

        <h2 id="optional-extensions">Optional extensions</h2>
        <div class="cards">
            <a class="card" href={`${base}/configuration/#optional-manifest`}>
                <strong>Manifest</strong>
                <span>Add special behavior only where you need it: exact status codes, delayed responses, failures, or explicit files.</span>
            </a>
            <a class="card" href={`${base}/proxy-panel/`}>
                <strong>Proxy panel</strong>
                <span>Expose multiple manifest scenarios per URL and switch between them directly in the browser.</span>
            </a>
        </div>
        <p>
            Both layers are optional. Start with local files and naming conventions, then introduce a manifest or the panel
            only when a request needs more control.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-a-request-is-resolved">How a request is resolved</h2>
        <ol>
            <li>The plugin checks whether the URL is inside the configured API prefix.</li>
            <li>If browser bypass is enabled, the request continues unchanged to the next Vite middleware or proxy.</li>
            <li>A matching automatic or selected scenario overrides endpoint settings; endpoint settings override root delay.</li>
            <li>If no explicit file applies, the plugin uses method-specific and generic naming-convention candidates.</li>
        </ol>
    </article>
</DocsLayout>
